---
import BaseLayout from '../../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="CagriSema: FDA Decision Pending — Nawtch"
  description="CagriSema's FDA decision is pending (Q4 2026) after Novo Nordisk's NDA filing — REDEFINE trial data and current status."
  canonicalPath="/supplements/peptides/cagrisema/"
  bodyClass="diets"
>
      <header>
        <div class="header-left">
          <h1>CagriSema</h1>
          <p class="subtitle">Semaglutide + cagrilintide combination — NDA filed, FDA decision pending late 2026.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="ghost-btn" href="/supplements/peptides/">← Back to Peptides</a>
      </header>

      <div class="settings-section" style="border:1px solid var(--accent); background:rgba(200,240,96,0.08);">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title" style="color:var(--accent);">Last Verified: August 14, 2026</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section" style="border:1px solid var(--accent3); background:rgba(240,160,96,0.08);">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title" style="color:var(--accent3); display:flex; align-items:center; flex-wrap:wrap; gap:8px;">
          Tier 2 — Investigational / Not Currently Approved <span style="display:inline-block; padding:2px 10px; border-radius:10px; font-size:11px; font-weight:700; text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:0.04em; background:rgba(96,200,240,0.15); color:var(--accent2);">Late-Stage Investigational — Pending Decision</span>
        </h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            CagriSema is <strong>not FDA-approved</strong>, but it's closer to approval than most
            entries on this page: Novo Nordisk has already filed for approval and a decision is
            expected within 2026. This page describes the mechanism and trial data — <strong>not dosing or usage guidance, and not a recommendation to use it.</strong> Talk
            to a doctor before considering it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            CagriSema is Novo Nordisk's combination of two peptides in one weekly injection:
            semaglutide (the GLP-1 receptor agonist behind Ozempic/Wegovy) and cagrilintide, an
            amylin receptor agonist. Amylin is a hormone co-released with insulin that also
            regulates appetite and gastric emptying through a separate pathway from GLP-1 — the
            combination is designed to produce a larger weight-loss effect than semaglutide alone
            by working through two complementary hormone systems at once.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">What the Trials Actually Show</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            The Phase 3 REDEFINE 1 trial showed an average of about 22.7% body weight loss at 68
            weeks among participants with obesity or overweight, with 91.9% of participants
            achieving at least 5% weight loss versus 31.5% on placebo. Novo Nordisk has publicly
            noted that in a head-to-head trial, CagriSema underperformed Eli Lilly's tirzepatide
            (Zepbound) on weight loss — a useful data point for calibrating expectations rather
            than treating it as automatically superior to existing approved options.
          </p>
          <br />
          <a href="https://www.clinicaltrialsarena.com/news/novo-nordisks-cagrisema-bested-by-lillys-zepbound-in-head-to-head-trial/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Clinical Trials Arena — Novo Nordisk's CagriSema Bested by Lilly's Zepbound in Head-to-Head Trial →</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">Current Regulatory Status — Pending Decision</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            Novo Nordisk submitted its New Drug Application to the FDA on December 18, 2025, for
            chronic weight management. As of this writing, no PDUFA date has been publicly
            confirmed, but company guidance points to an FDA decision in the fourth quarter of
            2026, consistent with the standard review clock for a new molecular entity. CagriSema
            remains <strong>not FDA-approved and not available by prescription</strong> until that
            decision is made. <strong>This is the entry on this page most likely to change status
            soon</strong> — check back once a decision is announced, since approval, rejection, or
            a delay are all live possibilities.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">Learn More</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <a href="https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/novo-nordisk-files-for-fda-approval-of-cagrisema-the-first-once-weekly-combination-of-glp1-and-amylin-analogues-for-weight-management-302645862.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Novo Nordisk — Novo Nordisk Files for FDA Approval of CagriSema</a>
        </div>
      </div>
</BaseLayout>
